      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Bachman family settled Freemansburg in the 1760s, building an old stone house that doubled as a tavern still standing on the western edge.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freemansburg%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Freemansburg’s boom began when the Lehigh Canal opened in the early 1800s, turning the town into a key river trade spot.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.boroughoffreemansburg.org/history",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "By the late 1800s, the Lehigh Valley Railroad eclipsed the canal, reshaping Freemansburg’s transportation scene.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.boroughoffreemansburg.org/history",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A bridge over the Lehigh River appeared in 1811, followed by the first physician in 1817 and a general store opening in 1825.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freemansburg%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "In 1838 a public school opened, and around the same time Jacob Freeman ran a local tavern that gave the town its name.",
